        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Nuclear weapons cannot be employed in compliance with the principle of distinction because they cannot be directed at a military objective without also attacking civilian objects.",
      "reasoning": "Protocol III of the Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons (CCW) prohibits attacks on a military objective located within a concentration of civilians by means of weapons that cannot be precisely targeted, unless the objective is clearly separated and feasible precautions are taken. Nuclear weapons are incapable of meeting these requirements, thus their use would breach the distinction rule of IHL.",
