          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ccw_p_iii",
          "article": "2(3)",
          "quoted_text": "It is further prohibited to make any military objective located within a concentration of civilians the object of attack by means of incendiary weapons other than air‑delivered incendiary weapons, except when such military objective is clearly separated from the concentration of civilians and all feasible precautions are taken"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Even the threat to use nuclear weapons is unlawful because it amounts to a threat to employ a weapon whose use would violate the prohibitions on indiscriminate attacks and excessive environmental damage.",
      "reasoning": "The Rome Statute criminalises not only the actual use but also the threat to employ weapons that are prohibited under its provisions. Since nuclear weapons are covered by the prohibitions on indiscriminate weapons (Article 8) and on attacks causing excessive environmental harm (Article 8(b)(iv)), a threat to use them is itself a war crime.",
